this is a meter he's Venezuelan and  seeking asylum in Spain earlier this  year Amador fled his home country and  arrived in Madrid he told us his story  suffered political persecution for  marching for protesting for being in  complete opposition to what's happening  at the moment I was threatened harassed  and robbed I left because I got sick and  also because of the political issues I  tried to stay in my home in my country  with my people  Emira arrived in Spain on a tourist visa  and didn't seek asylum upon arrival he  instead waited for five months I was  scared I asked about how asylum worked  what would come of it and what I  wouldn't be able to do what always  happens is they take your passport from  you you can't leave the country and you  don't know how long the process takes  you don't know what Pandora's box it  will open so it was for those reasons I  decided to take the time and seek  political asylum Spain's interior  ministry turned down our request for an  interview but official figures for 2017  have shown the vast majority of asylum  applications made by Venezuelans in  Spain were rejected or simply not  processed due to a large backlog of  applicants over twelve thousand  Venezuelans have sought asylum so far  this year here in Spain for various  cultural linguistic historical and  family reasons Spain is the number one  EU destination for Venezuelans fleeing  their country mario says who's better of  the United Nations refugee agency  explained the reality of the asylum  application process in Spain there are  cases of people waiting one or two years  and that includes Venezuelans one  process to stay in the country must be  cancelled or closed before another can  begin one person who's been in Spain  seeking international protection who's  been seeking asylum for two or three  years can then include  time in other processes for immigrants  to legalize themselves they can use it  to prove they have settled in Spain or  seek Spanish nationality  Lanna finale de Fora Mira  the legal limbo has been difficult but  he remains positive about his new life  in Madrid I don't know what's going to  happen sometimes I feel positive  sometimes I feel down sometimes I really  want to push forward but other times I  feel like I want to give up I sometimes  think of giving up my asylum application  but like everyone I have dreams I want  to come true I have goals and I think  Spain can allow me to achieve them  perhaps a small glimmer of hope for the  increasing number of Venezuelans seeking  asylum in Spain
 

